{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model consistently underestimates the magnitude of upward spikes (e.g., 74.25 to 76.67) and fails to capture the subsequent volatility, often predicting a flat or slowly declining path while true prices surge.",
    "Adjust for sentiment disconnect: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history frequently precede sharp price increases that the model misses; increase forecast slope when recent sentiment is 1.00 and history shows a breakout above 70.",
    "Reduce forecast ceiling after spikes: The model's long-term forecasts (30-day horizon) are too high relative to the realized mean, which tends to settle lower (around 72-74) after a spike; apply a downward bias of 2-4 EUR to the tail of the forecast.",
    "Account for overshoot correction: When true prices spike above 75, the model's subsequent predictions are too low; add a positive correction factor for the 5-10 day window following a price >75.",
    "Ignore flat-line inertia: The model exhibits excessive inertia, smoothing out the jagged nature of the true data; replace the model's smooth decay/growth with a more volatile trajectory that mirrors the 3-5 day rolling average of the true outcomes."
  ]
}
